    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-77804",
      "knowledge_point_id": "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-2$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ is not in the required domain $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})(-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$",
