    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-338755",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles",
      "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =10+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =10 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{3}{10})=(-17.457\\,603…)^{∘}≈-{17.458}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{17.458}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={342.542}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={342.542}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={17.458}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={17.458}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/821713cb7b71b2b15da5ecdf90c0e836234d280fa3753c71615b570017773672.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{17.458}^{∘} \\\\  & ={197.458}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{197}^{∘},\\,x≈{343}^{∘}.\n$$",
